This patch is not presented because it fixes any problems with the game
or adds new features.  It's mostly just to amuse players who are
already very skilled and want to try a somewhat more challenging, or at
least different, game.

It adds three new defines to config.h.  It provides a place where
people building the game can specify that harder monsters may be
generated earlier, whether to place more weight towards dungeon level
or player experience level in deciding the maximum monster difficulty
on a floor, and whether monster difficulty should also rise as a
function of game turns spent, and how quickly.  This is a very small
change to the game (the patch is less than 6k), but depending on which
are compiled in and how the numbers are changed, the resulting game may
require considerably different strategy.

Note that this patch does not affect preset monsters, only those
created by the game's random monster picker.  It also doesn't affect
the minimum difficulty of generated monsters, except in cases where the
minimum exceeds the maximum.  And late in the game, where there is a
lack of harder monsters to be generated, the game will probably be
largely the same as in vanilla, except maybe some games might generate
Archons when they might not otherwise.

The patch can be found in Pasi's patch database here:
http://bilious.homelinux.org/?235

For those unable to compile the game, three pre-built Windows versions
are provided.  They haven't been tested (but the patch itself has been
tested in several configurations).

http://www.hiddenglade.com/download/nh343-tw1.zip
In vanilla Nethack, dungeon level and player level are given equal
weight in determining the maximum difficulty of generated monsters.  In
this version, the different is more like 90% to 10%.  This should make
it more difficult to do a pacifist dive.

http://www.hiddenglade.com/download/nh343-tw2.zip
This one further displaces the importance of player level, enough so
that it plays no role at all.  However, generated monsters get harder
by one level of difficulty every 2000 turns that pass!  This should be
much more challenging -- indeed, it may be unplayable without a lucky
start.

http://www.hiddenglade.com/download/nh343-tw3.zip
This version displaces monster generation in the opposite direction
from the other patches.  Player level is much more of an influence on
monster difficulty than dungeon level (95/5 split).  Monsters at the
start receive a flat 3/5 of a level increase in difficulty.  It can be
to the player's advantage to play a pacifist game, although if the
player doesn't increase much in level then preset monsters will
probably eat him quickly.

I'd appreciate it if you'd let me know if you decide to try one or more
of these and your opinion on how it affects the game.

